    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d232">

                <pb facs="0312" n="214" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg214"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/341: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-08-31T14:00:00+08:00">August 31, 1932—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 31—2:51 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1037. I am informed that report of Commission of the League will be unanimous.
                    Report definitely finds that Japan was not justified by doctrine of self-defense
                    in doing what it did on September 18, 1931 (although it will carry a suggestion
                    that perhaps the subordinate officers on the spot may have so felt). The report
                    will find that Manchukuo could not have come into existence except with official
                    aid of Japanese military and authorities. Report will at its end carry
                    suggestion for settlement of problem thus brought about through negotiation of
                    treaties between China and Japan providing for reestablishment China’s
                    sovereignty in Manchuria, Government of Manchuria remaining autonomous under
                    authorities appointed with the assistance of League and advisers from foreign
                    countries.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
